@node Installation
@chapter Installation Instructions

This chapter describes a source-checkout installation.  Emacsvox requires
Emacs 31 or newer; use the same selected Emacs for configuration, compilation,
tests, and startup.

@menu
* Obtaining Emacsvox::         Obtaining Emacsvox.
* Quick Installation::         Configure, build, and start Emacsvox.
* Building And Testing The Speech Server::
@end menu

@node Obtaining Emacsvox
@section Obtaining Emacsvox

The maintained Emacsvox repository is
@url{https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox}.

@example
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox
cd emacsvox
@end example

@node Quick Installation
@section Quick Installation

@enumerate
@item
Select Emacs 31 or newer.  If it is not the system @command{emacs}, copy
@file{local.mk.example} to the ignored @file{local.mk} and set @code{EMACS}:

@example
EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs
@end example

Verify the selection before diagnosing a compiler failure:

@example
make check-emacs
@end example

@item
Configure and compile the checkout:

@example
make all
@end example

Do not compile Emacsvox with an older system Emacs.  Tests prefer source and
therefore do not prove that the byte-code used by a normal startup is current.

@item
Install the recommended Omnivox speech server and complete its audible smoke
test.  @xref{Speech Backends}, for the supported platform matrix, exact
prerequisite classes, source and WSL2 build paths, expected tone and speech,
and compatibility alternatives.  From the Emacsvox source tree, the supported
launcher first validates the selected Emacs and backend and then runs that
audible check:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --check
@end example

Continue only after hearing both the tone and speech.  Do not diagnose Emacs
configuration until this standalone Omnivox check passes.

@item
Prove a clean Emacsvox start:

@example
./bin/emacsvox
@end example

The launcher defaults to Omnivox.  It uses an explicit @env{EMACS} override,
then the @code{EMACS=} selection in @file{local.mk}, then
@command{emacs} on @env{PATH}.  It rejects Emacs older than 31, resolves the
checkout independently of the calling directory, and loads the setup source
by its absolute path.  The session uses @option{-Q}: it loads neither site
files nor a personal Emacs init file.  This makes the first successful speech
a reproducible installation proof rather than a side effect of existing
configuration.

@command{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} prints the selected root, setup source,
Emacs version, and backend without playing audio or starting interactive
Emacs.  @command{./bin/emacsvox --help} documents explicit @env{EMACS},
@env{TTS_PROGRAM}, and @env{EMACSVOX_DIR} overrides.  Arguments after
@option{--} are passed to Emacs.

@item
For normal customized starts, select the server before loading Emacsvox.  In a
shell startup file, set @env{TTS_PROGRAM} to the recommended server name:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

The equivalent Emacs form, evaluated before setup, is:

@lisp
(setenv "TTS_PROGRAM" "omnivox")
@end lisp

The retired DECtalk-named generic environment selector is not recognized; see
@file{etc/tts-modernization.org} for the migration table.  Names for actual
DECtalk servers, including @code{dtk-exp} and @code{dtk-soft}, remain valid
server choices.

Then, for a changing source checkout, load the setup source explicitly from
your Emacs init file:

@lisp
(load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

The portable launcher is intentionally an isolated first-use path; this init
file form owns repeat use with personal customization.  The setup entry point
can warn and prefer source when newer Lisp would
otherwise be hidden by stale byte-code.  A stable installed copy with current
byte-code may instead use:

@lisp
(load-library "emacsvox-setup")
@end lisp
@end enumerate

After an ordinary Lisp edit, run @command{make bytecode}.  After switching
branches, pulling, or changing Lisp macros or public interfaces, run
@command{make bytecode-rebuild}.  The non-mutating
@command{make bytecode-check} verifies that byte-code exists, is current, and
was produced by the selected Emacs.

@node Building And Testing The Speech Server
@section Building And Testing The Speech Server

Speech-server launchers live in @file{servers/}.  Build them through the
repository-root targets above so the selected paths and staging rules remain
consistent.

The Omnivox smoke test and its audible success condition are in
@ref{Selecting And Checking Omnivox}.  The following protocol-level test is
only for retained direct Tcl-compatible servers.  Run one of those launchers
from the repository root, for example:

@example
./servers/espeak
@end example

Then enter:

@example
q @{This is a test.@}
d
s
@end example

The @code{q} command queues text, @code{d} dispatches it, and @code{s} stops
speech.  Type @kbd{C-d} to close the server.  Backend-specific READMEs may
provide stronger diagnostics and should take precedence over this generic
smoke test.

From Emacs, use @code{M-x tts-speak-version} to verify the selected adapter and
@code{M-x tts-restart} after changing server configuration.  Omnivox also
records per-process diagnostic logs below
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}, defaulting to
@file{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}; spoken text is omitted unless explicit
text logging is enabled.
